Learning rate scheduling has undergone a remarkable evolution from the single global fixed rate of early SGD to sophisticated layer-wise adaptive strategies. This research systematizes this evolution into five generations: (Gen1) global fixed, (Gen2) global scheduling, (Gen3) parameter-level adaptation, (Gen4) layer-level differentiation, and (Gen5) joint layer-time scheduling. We trace the fundamental motivation behind each transition, showing how the shift from ``one-size-fits-all'' to ``tailoring by layer and time'' addresses the \emph{layer-wise update conflict} in transfer learning: lower layers require small updates to preserve general knowledge while higher layers need large updates to adapt to new tasks. Building on this taxonomy, we propose Discriminative Adaptive Layer Scaling (DALS), a framework integrating warmup-cosine scheduling, phase- and depth-aware Grokfast gradient filtering, and LARS-style trust ratios into a single optimizer. We benchmark 18 strategies including three DALS variants across all five generations on \hl{ten datasets spanning from-scratch and fine-tuning regimes: synthetic (MLP), CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100 (ConvNet), and RTE, TREC-6, IMDb, SST-2, and MRPC (DistilBERT and BERT-base, fine-tune)}. On the synthetic controlled benchmark, \hl{the adaptive and lookahead methods achieve the highest accuracies (up to 98.0\%), while DALS is competitive at 97.5\% and DALS-Fast reaches 90\% in just 3 epochs}. The cross-dataset analysis reveals regime-dependent patterns: strategies that perform well on from-scratch training (DALS, Fixed SGD) are less dominant on fine-tuning tasks, while adaptive methods (RAdam, Lookahead) excel on those benchmarks---no single strategy wins across all regimes. The STLR+Discriminative combination (the ULMFiT recipe) exhibits substantial degradation even on DistilBERT fine-tuning of TREC-6 (\hl{36.4\%}), indicating that its strong directional decay is sensitive to the pretrained-feature/label-alignment regime. The phase- and depth-aware design of DALS aims to avoid either extreme, \hl{delivering stable competitive accuracy across all ten datasets without the collapse observed for the discriminative family}. We frame the five-generation taxonomy as an organizational viewpoint rather than a definitive historical progression, and we discuss the limitations of the current experimental scale.
